
The raw silicon must be purified to extraordinary levels. Semiconductor manufacturing cannot tolerate ordinary industrial impurities because tiny defects can ruin transistors.

Manufacturers then grow the purified silicon into a large single-crystal ingot — essentially a long cylinder in which the silicon atoms are arranged consistently.

That cylinder is sliced into extremely thin disks. The disks are ground and polished until their surfaces are extraordinarily flat and smooth.

Only then are they ready for a fab such as TSMC.

4. Why does purity matter economically?

Imagine manufacturing a chip containing tens of billions of transistors. A microscopic contamination problem can create defective circuits and lower yield.

Yield = the percentage of manufactured chips that actually work well enough to sell.

This concept will become extremely important later. A fab can have theoretically excellent technology and still have poor economics if its yield is bad.

So although a blank wafer is much less valuable than the finished chips, wafer quality matters because defects introduced at the beginning can destroy value after expensive processing has already been performed.

5. Companies to know

The silicon-wafer market is concentrated. Important suppliers include:

- Shin-Etsu Chemical — Japan; one of the world’s largest semiconductor silicon-wafer suppliers.

- SUMCO — Japan; another major global supplier.

- GlobalWafers — Taiwan; major wafer producer with manufacturing across multiple regions.

- Siltronic — Germany; major European supplier.

- SK Siltron — South Korea; part of the broader SK ecosystem.

The key point: TSMC does not make everything itself. Before ASML, Applied Materials, Lam Research or KLA equipment ever touches the wafer, specialized materials suppliers have already done difficult manufacturing work.

6. Where is the economic moat?

At first glance, wafers sound commoditized: ‘It’s just silicon.’ That is misleading.

Leading semiconductor customers need extreme consistency, purity and defect control. Suppliers must qualify with fabs, and changing a critical material supplier introduces manufacturing risk.

However, wafer suppliers generally do not have an ASML-like monopoly. There are several credible producers, and wafer demand is tied to overall semiconductor production.

So the industry has barriers to entry, but less scarcity-driven pricing power than EUV lithography.

7. Is the wafer business cyclical?

Yes — importantly so.

Wafer demand ultimately follows semiconductor wafer starts. When chipmakers expect strong demand, they increase production and consume more wafers. When inventories build and chip demand weakens, utilization falls.

There can also be inventory corrections throughout the supply chain. Customers may over-order during shortages and later reduce purchases while working through inventory.

This makes wafer suppliers more exposed to the semiconductor cycle than a uniquely constrained supplier like ASML.

8. The investment lens

Here is the framework to start developing:

Being essential does not automatically mean capturing the most value.

Silicon wafers are absolutely essential. Without them there are no chips. But several companies can supply them.

EUV lithography is also essential, but only ASML can currently provide production EUV scanners.

That difference — essentiality versus scarcity — is one of the most important ideas for understanding where profits accrue in the AI supply chain.

When we eventually compare NVIDIA, TSMC, ASML, memory suppliers, networking companies and data-center operators, repeatedly ask:

- How essential is this layer?

- How many credible suppliers exist?

- How difficult is switching?

- How quickly can new capacity enter?

- Who actually has pricing power?

Remember these 5 things

- A wafer is the circular silicon substrate on which many chips are fabricated simultaneously.

- Leading-edge fabs primarily use 300 mm wafers.

- Semiconductor-grade silicon requires extraordinary purity and precision.

- Major suppliers include Shin-Etsu, SUMCO, GlobalWafers, Siltronic and SK Siltron.

- Wafer manufacturing has meaningful barriers to entry, but its economics are more cyclical and competitive than ASML’s EUV monopoly.

Quick check

Why can an input be absolutely essential to AI chips but still have weaker economics than another input?

Answer: because value capture depends not only on importance, but on scarcity, competition, switching costs and capacity constraints.

